WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:02.900
- [Narrator] John Shaffner
designed the most famous

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.418
New York City apartment of all time.

00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:07.950
Except it only had three walls

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.350
and no ceiling.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.800
And it wasn't actually in New York.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.650
- This is my apartment, and I like it!

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.400
- [Narrator] John Shaffner
designed the set of Friends.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.400
John is a production designer,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.520
and John has worked on a lot of shows.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.290
- I've done 44 series, and 68 pilots.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.650
“Dharma and Greg,” “The Drew Carey Show,”

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.310
“Two and a Half Men,” “Big Bang Theory.”

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.260
- [Narrator] And most notably, “Friends.”

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.600
- When I get assigned a new show,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.560
I do what is called a
sketch model in white paper.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.550
And you hold it up and you
close one eye, and you squint.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.009
And you can see just what
the camera is gonna see.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.638
- Hello.

00:00:52.638 --> 00:00:54.690
- When we were presenting “Friends,”

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.060
I presented a white model,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.130
and the producer looked at it and said,

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.490
"Well, now what are we
going to do about color?"

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.420
- What do you want to do?

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.614
- And I said, "I think we
should paint the set purple."

00:01:02.614 --> 00:01:03.670
- Ooh.
- Uh oh.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.470
- [John] Everybody was
really anxious about.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.700
- Nobody like change.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.410
- Until I painted the little model purple.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:10.503
- It's purple!

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.859
- [John] Color's really important
in terms of establishing

00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:14.162
the show identity.

00:01:14.162 --> 00:01:15.870
- Hey, ooh, slow down!

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.310
- When you switch to “Friends,”

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.000
you saw that it was purple,
and you stayed tuned.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.550
One of the responsibilities
of a production designer

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.626
is to look into the future, if you can.

00:01:26.626 --> 00:01:27.459
(screaming)

00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:30.380
We put a door all the way up this hallway

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.840
and we really didn't say where it went to.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.460
I said, "Why don't we just wait and see

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.000
"where the stories take us."

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.070
So all of a sudden, years into the show,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.620
we've never seen this door open.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.800
- I wasn't trying to open your closet,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.450
I wasn't trying to open
your closet, I swear!

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.800
- [John] They do an entire episode about

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.640
this was Monica's messy closet.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.909
- Oh my God!

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:49.905
(laughter)

00:01:49.905 --> 00:01:52.072
- (gasps)

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.610
- [John] I definitely think
that the coffee house on Friends

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.560
sort of influenced our culture of where

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.869
people like to meet and hang around.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:00.730
- Thank you.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.910
- [John] And now, of
course, 25 years later,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.709
there's a coffee house on every corner.

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:08.393
I'm surprised how many of
them have overstuffed chairs.

00:02:08.393 --> 00:02:10.260
- What am I going to do?

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.630
- In the pilot, the moment when Rachel

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.800
walks through the door
in her wedding dress

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.390
is a pivotable moment.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.710
We made a decision to put the
door up here on a diagonal.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.000
She walks, pow, through those doors

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.830
and straight to the camera

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:27.960
and everybody had to, like,
physically turn and look.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:28.793
- This is everybody.

00:02:28.793 --> 00:02:31.130
This is Chandler, and Phoebe, and Joey.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:32.930
And you remember my brother, Ross?

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:33.784
- Sure!

00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:34.617
- Hey.
- Hi.

00:02:34.617 --> 00:02:35.830
Oh!
(laughter)

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.790
- Who knew that that was
going to be the beginning

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.030
of 10 years of a number
one show on television?

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.580
Audiences relate to sitcoms
because once you get

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.950
to know the characters, you
become invested in their story.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.610
So it's easy to turn on and
watch an episode of Friends.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.300
It's a comfort coming back to
visit with these characters

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.890
and be with them for that half hour.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.000
I'm so lucky I got to design Friends.

